{
  "requirement": "Usage MUST record, for each failed provider request, its HTTP status and\nprovider error code when the provider reports them, and whether the harness\nretried it. It MUST NOT record the failure's message text.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.authoring.usage_requests.failures`.\n\n**Obligations derived from the text**\n1. Usage records the HTTP status of each failed request when the provider reports one.\n2. Usage records the provider error code when the provider reports one.\n3. Usage records whether the harness retried the request.\n4. Usage does not record the failure's message text.\n\n**Coverage**\n- **Status and code, end to end.** `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[throttled_requests_retry_unchanged_and_record_every_attempt]` uses `ThrottledProvider` with status 429, code `ThrottlingException` and the provider body \"Too many tokens\". It fails five requests, then succeeds. For each failed request it asserts that `failure` equals exactly `{\"http_status\":429,\"error_code\":\"ThrottlingException\",\"retried\":true}`. It also asserts that the successful request has no `failure` entry.\n- **Retried true and false.** The throttled test covers `retried: true`. `src/authoring/usage.rs#mod[tests]/fn[failed_submission_can_be_confirmed_on_a_later_attempt]` covers the transition. `end_request` records `retried: false`. After `log.retried(first)` the persisted value is `true`.\n- **Message text.** Both tests compare the whole `failure` object with `assert_eq!` against a three-key JSON value. A message field, or the \"Too many tokens\" body, would make that comparison fail. The provider in the throttled test does carry a message body, so the check is meaningful.\n- **Extraction of status and code.** `src/authoring/providers.rs#mod[tests]/fn[failure_codes_are_the_status_and_code_a_provider_reports]` checks that a provider error with status and code yields `(Some(429), Some(\"ThrottlingException\"))`. It also checks that a plain error and a `TimedOut` error with no provider report yield `(None, None)`. That covers the \"when the provider reports them\" condition.\n- **Error survives the SDK path.** `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[sdk_string_errors_preserve_habeas_provider_reports_and_error_kinds]` checks that the status 403 and the code `AccessDeniedException` still come out of `failure_codes` after the error passes through the SDK. It runs this for two error kinds.\n\n**Other tests**\n- `permanent_provider_failure_returns_without_waiting_or_retrying` and `a_request_failing_for_a_minute_fails_the_assessment` in `src/authoring/mod.rs` also assert the recorded `failure` object. They check that a non-retried failure records `retried: false` through the real conversation. They carry no `{test}` reference to this requirement. That is a marking gap, not an adequacy gap, because the marked tests already cover each obligation.\n\n**No gap established**\n- The `usage.rs#struct[RequestFailure]` fields are plain `Option` values copied through, so absent status and code involve no conditional of their own.\n- I found no input class or boundary in the requirement text that the tests miss.",
  "references": "d4d301e250bc44af228c2860c4136575d41362436ffdd50bd152059c7f70c3f3",
